<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- What one module is allowed to know about another: interfaces and DTOs, no
       implementations and no entities. A module references THIS, never another
       module's project — that single rule is what makes extracting a module a
       directory move instead of a redesign.

       ONE package reference, added in Phase 13.4, and the rule it has to satisfy
       is unchanged: when a module becomes its own service, this project is what
       the wire schema is generated from, so anything that could not survive a
       network hop does not belong in it. Mediator.Abstractions is marker
       interfaces and two handler contracts — IRequest<T>, INotification and
       their handlers — so a message type that implements one serialises exactly
       as it did before.

       It is declared HERE rather than in each of the six module csprojs, and
       that is deliberate rather than lazy: every module already references
       Contracts, so one pin flows to all of them and there is one version to
       move instead of seven. (The EF packages next door are named per project
       for the opposite reason — Npgsql declares a RANGE on EF Core, so a
       transitive-only reference lands on the range's floor. This package is
       nobody's transitive dependency but ours, so there is no floor to land on.)

       The upside is worth naming, because it retires an argument written down in
       SharedKernel/DomainEvents.cs at 13.3c: there was no IDomainEvent marker
       because Contracts may reference no other Jobkeep assembly, and a marker in
       SharedKernel would have forced one. A marker arriving from a PACKAGE has
       no such problem, so INotification is the constraint that could not be
       written by hand. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Jobkeep.Contracts</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Mediator.Abstractions" Version="3.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
